At midband, the coupling capacitors \(C_b\) and \(C_c\) act as short circuits and the emitter bypass capacitor \(C_e\) shorts the emitter to AC ground Hence the small-signal equivalent circuit is shown in the figure <img src="img/ed7e7c2bd50595ed.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,256px)"> Since the emitter is at AC ground \[V_{\pi}=V_s\] The collector current is \[I_c=g_mV_{\pi}\] The output voltage is \[V_o=-I_cR_C\] Substituting \(I_c=g_mV_{\pi}\) \[V_o=-g_mV_{\pi}R_C\] Since \(V_{\pi}=V_s\) \[V_o=-g_mR_CV_s\] Therefore \[A_v=\frac{V_o}{V_s}=-g_mR_C\] Now \[g_m=\frac{I_C}{V_T}
=\frac{1.3\times10^{-3}}{26\times10^{-3}}
=0.05\,\text{S}\] Hence \[A_v=-0.05\times2000=-100\] \[\boxed{A_v=-100}\]

With \(C_e\) removed, the emitter is no longer at AC ground <img src="img/20c62ed77301cb58.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,256px)"> Applying KVL in the input loop \[V_s=V_{\pi}+I_eR_E\] Since \[I_e=I_c+I_b
=\left(1+\frac{1}{\beta}\right)I_c
=\left(1+\frac{1}{\beta}\right)g_mV_{\pi}\] Therefore \[V_s=V_{\pi}\left[1+\left(1+\frac{1}{\beta}\right)g_mR_E\right]\] \[V_{\pi}
=\frac{V_s}{1+\left(1+\dfrac{1}{\beta}\right)g_mR_E}\] The output voltage is \[V_o=-I_cR_C
=-g_mV_{\pi}R_C\] Therefore \[A_v=\frac{V_o}{V_s}
=
-\frac{g_mR_C}
{1+\left(1+\frac{1}{\beta}\right)g_mR_E}\] Now \[g_m=\frac{I_C}{V_T}
=\frac{1.3\times10^{-3}}{26\times10^{-3}}
=0.05\,\text{S}\] \[A_v
=
-\frac{0.05\times2000}
{1+\left(1+\frac{1}{100}\right)(0.05)(500)}
=
-\frac{100}{26.25}
\approx-3.81\] \[\boxed{A_v\approx-3.81}\]

If \(C_b\) is short circuited, it behaves as a wire Since \(C_b\) is already considered as a short circuit for midband small-signal analysis, replacing it by an ideal short does not change the AC equivalent circuit Therefore the voltage gain remains \[A_v=-g_mR_C\] Using \[g_m=\frac{I_C}{V_T}
=\frac{1.3\times10^{-3}}{26\times10^{-3}}
=0.05\,\text{S}\] \[A_v=-0.05\times2000=-100\] Hence \[V_o=A_vV_s\] \[V_o=-100\times0.01\sin(\omega t)\] \[V_o=-\sin(\omega t)\,\text{V}\] \[\boxed{V_o=-\sin(\omega t)\,\text{V}}\]

Since \(\beta\) is very large, \(I_B\approx0\) Therefore, \(I_C\approx I_E\) The Thevenin voltage is \[V_{TH}
=\frac{R_2}{R_1+R_2}V_{CC}
=\frac{5}{10+5}\times15
=5\,\text{V}\] Applying KVL \[V_{TH}=V_{BE}+I_ER_E\] Hence \[I_E=\frac{V_{TH}-V_{BE}}{R_E}
=\frac{5-0.7}{430}
=10\,\text{mA}\] Since \[I_C\approx I_E\] \[I_C=10\,\text{mA}\] \[\boxed{(D)\;10\,\text{mA}}\] <img src="img/b312f58a2c3f4aca.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,448px)">

The characteristic equation of the system is \(s^3 + \alpha s^2 + ks + 3 = 0\). RH Table: \[\begin{array}{c|cc}
s^3 &amp; 1 &amp; k \\
s^2 &amp; \alpha &amp; 3 \\
s^1 &amp; \dfrac{\alpha k - 3}{\alpha} &amp; 0 \\
s^0 &amp; 3 &amp; 
\end{array}\] For a stable system, all elements in the first column must be strictly positive: <ul><li>From \(s^2\) row: \(\alpha &gt; 0\)</li><li>From \(s^1\) row: \(\dfrac{\alpha k - 3}{\alpha} &gt; 0\)</li></ul> Since \(\alpha &gt; 0\), the numerator must satisfy: \[\alpha k - 3 &gt; 0 \implies \alpha k &gt; 3\] \[\boxed{(B) \ \ \alpha &gt; 0\ \ , \ \ \alpha k &gt; 3 }\]

questions.question:
Answer Questions Q9.00.1, Q9.00.2 and Q9.00.3 using the following data. These three questions together carry a total of <strong>5 marks</strong>. A certain linear, time-invariant system has the state and output representation shown below: \[\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x}_1\\
\dot{x}_2
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
-2 &amp; 1\\
0 &amp; -3
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
x_1\\
x_2
\end{bmatrix}
+
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\
0
\end{bmatrix}u\] \[y=
\begin{bmatrix}
1 &amp; 1
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
x_1\\
x_2
\end{bmatrix}\]<br/><br/>Find the eigenvalues (natural frequencies) of the system.

questions.explanation:
Given, \[\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x}_1\\
\dot{x}_2
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
-2 &amp; 1\\
0 &amp; -3
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
x_1\\
x_2
\end{bmatrix}
+
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\
0
\end{bmatrix}u\] \[y=
\begin{bmatrix}
1 &amp; 1
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
x_1\\
x_2
\end{bmatrix}\] The system matrix is, \[A=
\begin{bmatrix}
-2 &amp; 1\\
0 &amp; -3
\end{bmatrix}\] Eigenvalues are obtained from, \[|sI-A|=0\] Therefore, \[\left|
\begin{array}{cc}
s+2 &amp; -1\\
0 &amp; s+3
\end{array}
\right|
=0\] \[(s+2)(s+3)=0\] \[s = -2, -3\] Hence, \[\boxed{\lambda_1=-2,\qquad \lambda_2=-3}\]

questions.question:
Answer Questions Q9.00.1, Q9.00.2 and Q9.00.3 using the following data. These three questions together carry a total of <strong>5 marks</strong>. A certain linear, time-invariant system has the state and output representation shown below: \[\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x}_1\\
\dot{x}_2
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
-2 &amp; 1\\
0 &amp; -3
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
x_1\\
x_2
\end{bmatrix}
+
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\
0
\end{bmatrix}u\] \[y=
\begin{bmatrix}
1 &amp; 1
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
x_1\\
x_2
\end{bmatrix}\]<br/><br/>If \(u(t)=\delta(t)\) and \(x_1(0^+)=x_2(0^+)=0\), find \(x_1(t)\), \(x_2(t)\) and \(y(t)\), for \(t&gt;0\).

questions.explanation:
Given, \[u(t)=\delta(t)\] \[x_1(0^+)=0,
\qquad
x_2(0^+)=0\] State transition matrix is given by, \[\phi(t)=\mathcal{L}^{-1}\left\{(sI-A)^{-1}\right\}\] First compute, \[sI-A
=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
s+2 &amp; -1\\
0 &amp; s+3
\end{array}\right]\] Determinant is, \[|sI-A|
=
(s+2)(s+3)\] Therefore, \[(sI-A)^{-1}
=
\frac{1}{(s+2)(s+3)}
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
s+3 &amp; 1\\
0 &amp; s+2
\end{array}\right]\] \[=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\dfrac{1}{s+2} &amp;
\dfrac{1}{(s+2)(s+3)}
\\ 0 &amp;
\dfrac{1}{s+3}
\end{array}\right]\] Using partial fractions, \[\frac{1}{(s+2)(s+3)}
=
\frac{1}{s+2}
-
\frac{1}{s+3}\] Taking inverse Laplace transform element wise, \[\phi(t)
=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
e^{-2t} &amp;
e^{-2t}-e^{-3t}
\\ 0 &amp;
e^{-3t}
\end{array}\right]\] Since, \(u(t)=\delta(t)\) and \(x(0^+)=0\), the state response is, \[x(t)=\phi(t)B\] Here, \[B=
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\
0
\end{bmatrix}\] Therefore, \[x(t)
=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
e^{-2t} &amp;
e^{-2t}-e^{-3t}
\\ 0 &amp;
e^{-3t}
\end{array}\right]
\left[\begin{array}{c}
1\\
0
\end{array}\right]\] \[=
\left[\begin{array}{c}
e^{-2t}
\\ 0
\end{array}\right]\] Hence, \[\boxed{x_1(t)=e^{-2t}}\] \[\boxed{x_2(t)=0}\] Output equation is, \[y(t)=x_1(t)+x_2(t)\] Therefore, \[y(t)=e^{-2t}+0\] \[\boxed{y(t)=e^{-2t}}\]

